{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-300758",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
